-- 381: SOMETHING GROWS UP HIGH (rome, 2026-11-10). The high mountain, the fourth
-- tier, had no grazing of its own beyond the three rooms of mig 340, so the
-- grazers lived low. Fifteen of its eighty rooms now say something grows in
-- them: on the crest, at the foot of the ice, in the red ground, on the fan and
-- under the swept ground. They are grazing ground with the rest of the green
-- rooms (mountain-ground.ts). Only the words change here; the source is
-- regions/mountain-4.rooms, changed with this.

UPDATE rooms SET description = 'The permanent ice ends and rock begins, and the rock is a step up onto the crest of a ridge that runs away east as far as you can see it. Both sides fall away immediately. From this step on there is no such thing as walking round anything. The step itself has only lately come out from under the ice, and moss has taken every crack in it, a dark wet mat a finger deep. Something has been cropping it.'
  WHERE id = 'the-crest-step';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'A step off the north side of the ridge onto a ledge the size of a table, which is the only place on this whole crest a person can sit down. It ends in nothing. Birds have been using it: the rock is white with them, and there are pellets, and the pellets are full of small bones. What they leave feeds the ledge: along the foot of the wall a moss has come up thick as turf, greener than anything has a right to be up here, and bitten short along its outer edge.'
  WHERE id = 'the-north-drop';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'The crest broadens, loses its edge, and dies into a shoulder of broken rock. After a mile of having nothing on either side of you, ground that goes on in more than one direction is briefly disorienting, and you will stand here longer than you need to. In among the broken blocks, out of the wind, moss has got a hold in tight cushions the size of a fist, and most of them are bitten flat.'
  WHERE id = 'the-ridge-end';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'The end of the ice, filthy with the rock it has carried, giving back the things it took in at the top — stones, gravel, a scatter of bone, all of it in the same condition it went in and none of it from any animal that has been up here in living memory. This is the only place the ice and the red ground touch. Where the meltwater spreads over the gravel the ice has put down, a thin moss has started, bright against the dirt, and it is grazed to the stones.'
  WHERE id = 'the-glacier-toe';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'A channel of grey stone under the lip of the ice, running with meltwater in the afternoon and dry by dark, floored in silt so fine it takes a print like wax. The prints in it now are from this morning. There are three sets and they are all going the same way. Along both edges of the channel, where the silt stays wet through the night, a line of moss has taken, with a creeping willow no taller than your thumb threaded through it, nibbled back to the wood.'
  WHERE id = 'the-melt-run';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'A nose of solid rock standing out of the scree, the only thing on this fan that did not arrive by falling. It splits what comes down into two streams, and everything on one side of it goes west and everything on the other goes east, and the sorting the whole fan does starts here. In its lee, where nothing coming down can reach, a mat of moss and creeping willow has grown the shape of the rock''s shadow, and it has been eaten back to the stems.'
  WHERE id = 'the-boulder-nose';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'Where the fan flattens out and stops sorting, and everything that got this far lies mixed together: chips, splinters, one antler, half a jaw. Water runs under it rather than over it and you can hear that, faintly, through the stones. Where the sound is loudest the stones are green underneath and moss has come up between them, low and wet, cropped wherever a mouth can reach it.'
  WHERE id = 'the-lower-fan';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'Where water has run down the red rock for long enough to stain a stripe darker than the ground either side, twenty feet wide and running the whole length of the slope. It is dry now. It is dry for eleven months and it moves the entire hillside in the twelfth. Down the middle of the stripe, where the last of the wet stays longest, a dark moss holds on in a crust, and after the water it comes up green for a month. It is cropped the whole length.'
  WHERE id = 'the-stained-run';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'A gully cut deep into the red, walls above head height, floor of loose stone, and everything that comes off this side of the mountain funnels into it. Down here you can see nothing but the two walls and the strip of sky. Something has been sleeping against the west wall, in the dry, and the shape of it is still in the grit. On the east wall, in the one place the afternoon sun gets to, a seep comes out of the rock and there is moss under it all the way to the floor, bitten off as high as a hind can lift her head.'
  WHERE id = 'the-red-gully';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'A tower of rock standing on the ridge like a post, twice your height, that everything has to go round. There is a worn line round its base on the south side where everything has gone round it, and the wear is deep enough to be a path in its own right. Nothing has ever gone round it on the north. On that south side, in the sun and out of the wind, the foot of the tower is green with moss packed into the wear in cushions, and they are grazed level with the rock.'
  WHERE id = 'the-gendarme';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'A slab the size of a barn door that came off the ridge and did not fall — it leaned against what it came off and stopped, leaving a triangular gap underneath, dry and dark and out of the wind. There are old droppings in the back of it, dried to nothing, and a smell that has almost gone. At the mouth of the gap, where the drip off the slab lands, a fringe of moss and small tough leaves has come up in what the droppings left, and it is nibbled short.'
  WHERE id = 'the-lean';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'The ice turns a corner round a buttress of rock, and turning has torn it: the whole flank is a fan of cracks, none of them wide, all of them deep. Walking it is a matter of stepping over things you cannot see the end of, one after another, for longer than is comfortable. The buttress itself stands clear of the ice and takes the sun, and its ledges are furred with moss and grey lichen as far up as you can see, the lowest of them stripped.'
  WHERE id = 'the-glacier-bend';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'Frost has broken the rock into slabs with straight cracks between them, laid out in a pattern that looks made from a distance and looks like nothing at all from on top of it. Water gets in the cracks, freezes, and takes the rock apart another notch, every night of every year, forever. Down in the cracks, a hand''s depth out of the wind, it is another country: moss and small ferny leaves, green in every slot, bitten off wherever they reach the top.'
  WHERE id = 'the-pavement';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'A terrace under the swept ground, lower and out of the main of the wind, where the loose stuff the pavement sheds collects in a strip along the back wall. It is the last ground on this tier with any shelter in it at all, and it is where anything that intends to be up here for a night is going to be. In that strip the grit has made something like soil, and moss and a creeping willow have taken the length of the wall, grazed to the roots.'
  WHERE id = 'the-cold-terrace';
UPDATE rooms SET description = 'A shelf under the approach, wide and level, that ought to be a nesting ledge — it faces the sun, it is out of the wind, it has a wall at its back. It has been used as one. The old nests are still on it, three of them, packed with sticks carried up from four tiers down, and every one of them is empty and has been for years. What the nests left has rotted down into the cracks of the shelf, and moss has come up out of it thick and green along the foot of the wall, cropped close.'
  WHERE id = 'the-shelf-under';
